# Choosing an adviser, and checking a proposal

Hiring an adviser is a decision, and so is approving a proposal. Each has a structure like any other. The questions below test the proposal in front of you and the adviser's own decision process. They apply to consultancies, agencies, vendors and in-house teams, and they take one meeting.

## A proposal is a decision

A proposal packages a choice, the evidence behind it and the plan to carry it out. It is a consolidated decision, and it can be checked against the six primitives before you approve it. A plan that lacks a primitive will fail, in the way the proof defines. A proposal that shows clear evidence of the six has shown its structure. One that shows none leaves you unable to tell whether its authors possess them.

## The proposal check

<img src="images/07-proposal-check.png" alt="The six questions of the proposal check" width="800">

Put these to any proposal in front of you, and to its authors.

1. Objective. Does it state at least two explanations for the results it rests on, and the evidence that would separate them?
2. Risks. Does it name the actions that cannot be undone, and rule them out?
3. Exploration. Does it set aside effort for finding something better than the current plateau, and say who may authorise the short-term cost?
4. Policies. Does it say when the evidence will be treated as settled, and what would reopen the decision?
5. Constraints. Does it list the limits that cannot be traded, and say how options that breach them are removed before alternatives are compared?
6. Updating. Does it say what will tell its authors that the world has changed, and what they will do then?

These are the questions of the [self-check](self-check.md), turned onto a document.

## Why names are no longer enough

Names, years of experience and a case study from another company are the signals most advice is bought on. The [Dossier](https://decisional-integrity.github.io/Field/dossier/) collected the record on them. A peer-reviewed study of KPMG in Germany (Entry 14) found that after failures in KPMG's non-audit work, its audit clients suffered negative capital-market reactions while KPMG itself faced no significant consequences, and its authors conclude that reputation may be insufficient to restrain Big Four auditors where the risk of litigation is low. In an IBM-sponsored survey of 1,002 senior executives, 65% said traditional consulting no longer delivers real value (HFS Research, 2025-11-11; the Dossier rates this source Low-Medium for independence). The field puts the principle plainly: a credential shows what someone was trained in, and a referral shows that someone is willing to vouch. Neither shows that the person can do the work. The field applies the same standard to itself: its roles are held on proof, in public artefacts.

## When a case study counts

<img src="images/08-case-study-six-points.png" alt="Six points a case study must match to count as evidence for you" width="800">

A case study is evidence for you when your situation matches it on six points.

1. Objective. The same goal, unclear in the same way about what is working.
2. Risks. The same irreversible actions in play.
3. Exploration. The same plateau, with a better region available.
4. Policies. The same settled evidence, and a similar point of commitment.
5. Constraints. The same hard limits.
6. Updating beliefs. The same change in the world, with similar evidence arriving.

A case that matches on one or two shows what happened in a different situation. A case that matches on all six is evidence for you. This is Literacity's test, drawn from the way the necessity results are stated, one situation at a time.

## What the adviser test looks at

The field's published criteria describe six capabilities that every decision structure needs. For each, an adviser can name the mechanism that performs it and show one dated record where that mechanism changed a decision. A name, a client list and years of experience show what an adviser has been through. A mechanism and a dated record show what its process does today.

## The six questions

Put each question to the adviser, and ask for client details to be removed from the examples.

1. Objective Tracking. How does your process separate two explanations of the same results that point to different actions? Show me an engagement where evidence arrived and both your explanation and your recommendation changed.
2. Cross-Context Safety Transfer. Which actions do you treat as irreversible for a client, and where is that list kept when you recommend? Show me a recommendation you stopped or withdrew because of it.
3. Global Attractor Exploration. What standing allocation goes to moves that cost short-term results in order to reach a better position, and who can authorise that cost? Show me one taken after performance was already good.
4. Policy Simplification. At what point does your process treat the evidence as settled, and what reopens it? Show me a recurring decision where resources consolidated once it was.
5. Feasibility Projection. Which limits are non-tradeable in your process, and how are options that breach them removed before alternatives are compared? Show me an option that scored well and was refused.
6. Feedback Adaptation. How does your process notice that the world has changed, and what triggers a revisit of earlier assumptions? Show me assumptions revised, with dates, and later advice that differs because of it.

## What a good answer contains

A good answer names a specific mechanism, points to a dated record, and gives you a record that someone other than the adviser can read. The field's [general criteria](https://decisional-integrity.github.io/Field/standard/general-criteria/) treat the operation of a mechanism, shown in records of decisions, as the unit of evidence. A mechanism can carry a different name from the primitive it serves, so the test is the function. The [self-check](self-check.md) lists the stand-ins that most often appear in place of a mechanism, such as "we do risk evaluation" with no named unrecoverable actions.

